---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { legalAddress, legalCompany, legalMailto } from "../lib/legal";
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy — Zenguy"
  description="How NIESAYO GROUP, S.L. processes personal data for Zenguy under the GDPR and Spain’s LOPDGDD."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  effective="1 September 2026"
>
  <p>
    This Privacy Policy explains how {legalCompany.legalName} (“we”, “us”),
    acting under the trade name {legalCompany.tradeName}, processes personal
    data when you use {legalCompany.site}, {legalCompany.app}, the iOS app, or
    related services (browser tests, uptime monitoring, incidents, alerts and
    billing).
  </p>
  <p>
    It is the layered information required by articles 13 and 14 of Regulation
    (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) and Organic Law 3/2018 (LOPDGDD). It is not a contract
    of adhesion for extra processing: account data needed to provide Zenguy
    rest on the contract; marketing and optional analytics storage rest on
    separate consent.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Controller</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Controller</dt>
    <dd>{legalCompany.legalName}</dd>
    <dt>NIF</dt>
    <dd>{legalCompany.nif}</dd>
    <dt>Address</dt>
    <dd>{legalAddress}</dd>
    <dt>Email</dt>
    <dd><a href={legalMailto}>{legalCompany.email}</a></dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    We have not appointed a data protection officer because our core activities
    do not require one under article 37 GDPR. You can still write to{" "}
    <a href={legalMailto}>{legalCompany.email}</a> for any privacy request.
    You may also complain to the Spanish Data Protection Agency (AEPD),
    {" "}<a href="https://www.aepd.es">www.aepd.es</a>, or to another EU
    supervisory authority where you live or work.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Roles: controller and processor</h2>
  <p>
    For your account, workspace membership, billing, support and security logs,
    we are the <strong>controller</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For personal data that appear inside the sites you ask us to test or
    monitor (for example a name on a checkout screenshot, a customer email in
    a flow, or a secret you store), <strong>you are the controller</strong> and
    we act as your <strong>processor</strong> under article 28 GDPR. You must
    have a lawful basis to involve us, must not send special-category data
    unless strictly necessary and lawful, and must configure tests so they
    avoid unnecessary personal data. Processor terms are in the{" "}
    <a href="/terms/">Terms of Service</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Data we process</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Account data:</strong> name, email, password hash, verification
      status, legal acceptances (terms, privacy acknowledgement, marketing
      choice and version).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Workspace data:</strong> workspace name, members, roles,
      invitations, settings and time zone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Service configuration:</strong> browser-test instructions, start
      URLs, schedules, HTTP monitors, alert channels (email, phone number,
      Slack/Discord webhooks), and encrypted secrets you store.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run evidence:</strong> logs, screenshots, network diagnostics,
      Markdown failure reports and incident records, retained for 30 days.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Billing data:</strong> Stripe customer and subscription
      identifiers, invoices, usage of runs and prepaid alert credit. Card
      details are collected by Stripe, not stored by us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Device and security data:</strong> IP address, user agent,
      approximate location derived from IP, session tokens, app version, and
      diagnostic logs needed to secure the service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Authenticated service activity:</strong> when a signed-in user
      uses the web application or iOS app, our first-party service records the
      internal account identifier, web/app source, event type and time, a
      normalized page or screen category, and—where the action concerns one—
      the relevant workspace or resource identifier. This operational record
      is stored by Zenguy in Cloudflare D1; it is separate from optional Google
      Analytics, does not use Google cookies and is used for account-level
      activity, support, security, reliability and aggregate product-usage
      reporting. Automated checks and test outcomes are kept separate from
      human product-use metrics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consent-based analytics:</strong> only after you consent on the
      relevant origin, Google Analytics processes a first-party client
      identifier, session information, page interactions, page category and
      title, approximate geolocation, and browser, device and language
      information. Public-site page and referrer URLs have query parameters and
      URL fragments removed. We may pass only validated, low-cardinality
      campaign source, medium, name and CTA-location values separately. The
      application sends only allow-listed route templates, not actual workspace
      or resource URLs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web-application analytics milestones:</strong> completed registration,
      subscription checkout started, and a confirmed paid subscription. The
      confirmed subscription event includes product, price, currency and a
      Stripe invoice identifier as the transaction identifier. Analytics events
      exclude form contents, email addresses, tokens, test URLs, and workspace
      or resource identifiers. For a signed-in user who has consented, the
      browser derives a purpose-specific pseudonymous Google Analytics User-ID
      from the opaque internal account identifier. The internal identifier is
      not sent to Google, and the derived value is not based on an email, name
      or other contact data. This lets consented activity be counted more
      consistently across browsers or devices. We also send finite account-age
      and workspace-count bands and, for the current workspace, role and
      subscription-status categories. We do not send card details.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Communications:</strong> emails we send (verification, password
      reset, operational alerts, and, if you opt in, product updates) and
      support messages you send us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>iOS app:</strong> Face ID stays on the device (we do not receive
      biometric templates). When you enable push, we process the Expo push
      token, an internal device record, device model, platform and app version.
      The signed-in iOS app also sends first-party app-open events and
      normalized screen/resource visits as described above; it contains no
      Google Analytics SDK and does not send mobile activity to Google.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remote-AI consent:</strong> for each workspace, the provider,
      policy version, acceptance or revocation time, and the Owner or Admin who
      made the choice. The option starts off and is not inferred from a general
      acceptance of this policy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not sell personal data. We do not build advertising profiles.</p>

  <h2>4. Purposes and legal bases</h2>
  <p>Each purpose has its own article 6 GDPR basis:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Create and administer the account, workspaces and the service</strong>
      — performance of the contract (art. 6.1.b). Providing name, email and a
      password is necessary; without them we cannot open an account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run the tests and monitors you configure, store evidence, and
      send operational alerts to destinations you add</strong>
      — contract (art. 6.1.b). For phone, SMS or WhatsApp destinations we also
      need the recipient’s permission, which you confirm when you create the
      channel (consent, art. 6.1.a, as regards that recipient).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Billing, invoicing, tax and accounting</strong>
      — contract (art. 6.1.b) and legal obligation (art. 6.1.c), including the
      Spanish Commercial Code and General Tax Law retention duties.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Security, abuse prevention, debugging and service reliability</strong>
      — legitimate interests (art. 6.1.f): keeping the service safe and
      available for you and other customers. You may object under article 21
      GDPR.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Record authenticated use needed to operate the account and
      provide support</strong> — performance of the contract (art. 6.1.b), and
      for service reliability, abuse prevention and aggregate product
      improvement, our legitimate interests (art. 6.1.f). This first-party
      record is not optional Google Analytics and does not enable advertising.
      You may object to processing based on legitimate interests under article
      21 GDPR.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run browser tests with the OpenAI API</strong> — the
      workspace&apos;s explicit consent (art. 6.1.a). Zenguy executes browser
      tests on an isolated runner in Cloudflare Containers that uses the OpenAI
      API to choose and assess steps. Consent is off by default, requires an
      affirmative action by an Owner or Admin, and can be withdrawn from AI data
      sharing at any time. Without a current consent no browser test runs in
      that workspace; withdrawal stops future runs, and a run already in
      progress may finish.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deliver a consistent, readable web interface</strong> — legitimate
      interests (art. 6.1.f). Google Fonts receives the technical request data
      needed to deliver the configured typefaces on the public site and web
      application. This is separate from optional Google Analytics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Comply with law, respond to authorities, and handle claims</strong>
      — legal obligation (art. 6.1.c) or legitimate interests (art. 6.1.f).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Product emails about Zenguy</strong> (not operational alerts) —
      consent (art. 6.1.a and art. 21 LSSI-CE). The checkbox is optional,
      unchecked by default, and can be withdrawn at any time by writing to{" "}
      <a href={legalMailto}>{legalCompany.email}</a>. Consent is not a
      condition of creating an account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure and improve Zenguy with Google Analytics</strong> — your
      consent (art. 6.1.a GDPR and art. 22.2 LSSI-CE). On the public site this
      means visits, page use, campaigns and CTA interactions; in the application
      it means normalized route categories, the pseudonymous consented-account
      identifier and the limited product milestones and categories described
      above. Analytics remains off on an origin if you reject it or have not
      chosen there.
      Consent is optional and can be withdrawn using <strong>Cookie settings</strong>
      in the public-site footer or <strong>Cookie preferences</strong> in
      the application, without limiting access to Zenguy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not take solely automated decisions with legal or similarly
    significant effects (art. 22 GDPR).
  </p>

  <h2>5. Recipients and processors</h2>
  <p>
    We only share data with providers that help us operate Zenguy, under a
    contract, and only as needed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong> and its EEA entity — hosting (Workers,
      Pages), database (D1), object storage (R2), DNS, email sending and
      security.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stripe, Inc. / Stripe Payments Europe, Limited</strong> —
      subscriptions, invoices, tax and the customer portal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Twilio Inc.</strong> — SMS and voice operational alerts you
      configure. We do not share mobile numbers with anyone for their
      marketing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OpenAI, LLC</strong> — model inference for browser-test execution
      on the Cloudflare Containers runner, and only after current workspace consent.
      OpenAI may receive the test name, instructions, target URLs, device
      configuration, relevant page content and screenshots, console/network
      results, and model input/output needed to choose and assess steps. It does
      not receive account, member, billing or notification data. Zenguy never
      releases configured secret values to the runner or to OpenAI; only
      placeholder names remain in the instructions. OpenAI states that API
      inputs and outputs are not used to train its models by default. Unless
      our OpenAI project has an approved shorter retention control, standard
      API abuse-monitoring logs may contain customer content for up to 30 days.
      See OpenAI&apos;s <a href="https://openai.com/business-data/">business-data commitments</a>
      and <a href="https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/default-usage-policies-by-endpoint">API data controls</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expo (650 Industries, Inc.)</strong> — iOS push delivery and app
      updates, when you use the iOS app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Ireland Limited</strong> — Google Analytics reporting for
      the public marketing site and application, only after consent on the
      relevant origin. We configure Google signals and advertising
      personalisation as disabled. Google also delivers the web fonts used by
      the public site and web application independently of Analytics consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Slack, Discord or other destinations <em>you</em> choose as alert
      webhooks — those providers then process the message under their terms.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We may also disclose data if required by a court or authority, or to
    professional advisers bound by confidentiality.
  </p>

  <h2>6. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Some providers (notably Cloudflare, Stripe, Twilio, OpenAI, Expo and Google)
    may process data in the United States or other countries outside the EEA.
    Where that happens we rely on an adequacy decision, the EU–US Data Privacy
    Framework if the provider is certified, and/or the European Commission’s
    Standard Contractual Clauses, plus supplementary measures where needed
    (GDPR Chapter V).
  </p>
  <p>
    Google publishes <a href="https://business.safety.google/intl/es/adsdatatransfers/">information about the transfer mechanisms</a>
    it may use for its advertising and analytics products, including the
    EU–US Data Privacy Framework in certain circumstances and Standard
    Contractual Clauses for certain other transfers.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Retention</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Account and workspace records: while the account is open.</li>
    <li>
      After deletion: we keep billing and tax records for up to six years, as
      Spanish commercial and tax law requires, and security logs for up to
      twelve months unless a claim needs them longer.
    </li>
    <li>Run evidence (screenshots, reports, logs): 30 days.</li>
    <li>
      OpenAI request/response content of consented workspaces follows the provider control
      described above: standard abuse-monitoring retention is up to 30 days
      unless a shorter approved control applies. The workspace consent record
      remains while the workspace is active and for as long as reasonably
      necessary to demonstrate the choice; actor identifiers are removed when
      the relevant account is deleted.
    </li>
    <li>
      Authenticated service activity: high-volume page, screen and view events
      for up to 90 days; other account and product activity events for up to
      365 days, unless a shorter account-deletion flow or a longer legal or
      security need applies.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google Analytics user-level and event-level data: 14 months. Google’s
      retention control does not affect its standard aggregated reports, which
      may remain available for longer. See Google’s <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/7667196">data-retention documentation</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The analytics preference: up to 12 months from your choice on zenguy.com
      and www.zenguy.com, and up to 24 months on app.zenguy.com. Each origin
      stores its decision independently and requests it again when that period
      expires. Google Analytics cookies, when consented to, have a default
      expiry of up to two years but can be removed earlier when you withdraw
      through the relevant preference control or through your browser.
    </li>
    <li>Marketing consent records: until you withdraw consent, plus evidence of the consent for the period we may need to demonstrate it (art. 7.1 GDPR).</li>
    <li>
      If you never verify the email, the pending account and its verification
      token expire on the schedule described in the product (verification
      tokens last 24 hours; unverified accounts may be removed as abuse
      prevention).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. Account and workspace deletion</h2>
  <p>
    The iOS app provides <strong>Account → Delete my account</strong>. It asks
    for the current password, the word DELETE and a final confirmation; it does
    not require contacting support. Deletion immediately revokes sessions and
    push tokens, removes memberships in workspaces owned by other people,
    revokes pending invitations, makes every owned workspace inaccessible,
    cancels its service subscription and starts the retryable purge of its
    database and object-storage data. The account is replaced with an
    anonymized tombstone so retained financial and security records no longer
    identify an active user.
  </p>
  <p>
    The common deletion path completes immediately. If a billing provider or
    storage operation is temporarily unavailable, the workspace remains
    inaccessible and the deletion job retries until the purge succeeds. The
    only exceptions are billing/tax records kept for up to six years and
    necessary security records kept for up to twelve months or longer when a
    legal claim requires it. See <a href="/privacy-choices/">Privacy choices</a>
    for the controls available before and after deletion.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Security</h2>
  <p>
    We use TLS, hashed passwords, encrypted secrets, access control by
    workspace role, short-lived sessions, rate limits and audit logs. No
    online service is perfectly secure. If a personal-data breach is likely to
    result in a risk to you, we will notify the AEPD within 72 hours where
    required (art. 33 GDPR) and we will inform you when art. 34 GDPR requires
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You may request access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability
    and objection, and you may withdraw consent without affecting earlier
    lawful processing (arts. 15–21 GDPR, arts. 12–18 LOPDGDD). You also have
    the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions (art. 22).
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the controls listed at <a href="/privacy-choices/">Privacy choices</a>
    or write to <a href={legalMailto}>{legalCompany.email}</a>. We may need to
    verify your identity. We will answer within one month, extendable as the
    GDPR allows. You can also complain to the AEPD.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Spain, users aged 14 or over may generally consent to information-society
    services (art. 7 LOPDGDD). Zenguy is a business service: the contract
    requires that you are 18 or older and able to bind yourself or your
    organisation.
  </p>

  <h2>11. SMS and phone alerts</h2>
  <p>
    Operational SMS and calls are only sent to numbers you enrol, after an
    explicit, unchecked confirmation in the product. We do not sell mobile
    numbers or SMS opt-in data, and we do not share them with third parties
    for their marketing. Recipients can reply STOP to opt out of SMS from the
    sending number, or HELP for help. Consent to SMS is not a condition of
    buying Zenguy. Frequency follows the monitors you configure and may include
    several messages in a day when something is down.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Children</h2>
  <p>Zenguy is not directed at children and we do not knowingly collect their data.</p>

  <h2>13. Changes</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy when the service or the law changes. The date at
    the top is the current version. Material changes will be announced in the
    product or by email when appropriate. Continued use after the effective
    date of a change that does not require new consent means you have been
    informed of that change.
  </p>
</Legal>
